    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-r/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A mayoral candidate, Hans Van Dyke, claims that 40% of the electoral will vote for him in the next election. In a recent opinion poll of 20 recently selected voters it was found that only 4 people will vote for Hans Van Dyke. a) Test, at the 5% level of significance whether, or not, the opinion poll supports Hans Van Dyke’s claim. ( ) 7 In a second opinion poll of n randomly selected people, it was found that no one will be voting for Hans Van Dyke. As a result of this poll, Hans Van Dyke’s claim is rejected at 1% significance. b) Determine the smallest value of n . ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:578.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let p be the share of voters supporting Hans and test H0 : p = 0.4 against H1 : p &lt; 0.4. (a) Under H0, X ∼B(20, 0.4). [B1] [A1] The observed count 4 has a lower-tail probability P(X ≤4) ≈0.05095195. [M1] [A1] This slightly exceeds 0.05, so fail to reject H0. [M1] [A1] The poll gives insufficient evidence that his support is below 40% . [A1] (b) With zero supporters the p-value is 0.6n. [B1] Require 0.6n &lt; 0.01. Taking logs and dividing by log(0.6) &lt; 0 gives n &gt; log(0.01) log(0.6) ≈9.015. [M1] Check n = 9, which gives 0.010077696, and n = 10, which gives 0.0060466176. Only the latter is below 0.01.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The minimum is 10 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:205.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A mayoral candidate, Hans Van Dyke, claims that 40% of the electoral will vote for him in the next election. In a recent opinion poll of 20 recently selected voters it was found that only 4 people will vote for Hans Van Dyke. a) Test, at the 5% level of significance whether, or not, the opinion poll supports Hans Van Dyke’s claim. ( ) 7 In a second opinion poll of n randomly selected people, it was found that no one will be voting for Hans Van Dyke. As a result of this poll, Hans Van Dyke’s claim is rejected at 1% significance. b) Determine the smallest value of n . ( ) 3"
  },
