    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-r/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A geologist is looking for fossils in rocks. In a certain area it has been established over a long period of time that 10% of the rocks contain fossils. The geologist selects twenty rocks from this area. a) State 2 conditions that must be apply in order for a binomial model to be valid. ( ) 2 Find the probability that in the geologist’s sample there will be … b) … one rock containing fossils. ( ) 2 c) … at least one rock containing fossils. ( ) 2 The geologist selects a new sample of n rocks.\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q5-question-2.svg\" alt=\"He wants to have at least a 95% chance that his new sample will contain fossils. d) Determine the smallest value of n . ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:585.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Each selected rock has the same fossil-bearing probability, and fossil outcomes for different rocks are independent: [B1] [A1] (b) With X ∼B(20, 0.1), P(X = 1) = 20(0.1)0.919 ≈0.2702 . [M1] [A1] (c) P(X ≥1) = 1 −0.920 ≈0.8784 . [M1] [A1] (d) The complementary event is no fossil in any rock, with probability 0.9n. Rearrange to 0.9n ≤0.05; as log 0.9 &lt; 0, division reverses the inequality: The requirement 1 −0.9n ≥0.95 gives n ≥log(0.05)/ log(0.9) ≈28.43, [B1] [A1] hence 29 . [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A geologist is looking for fossils in rocks. In a certain area it has been established over a long period of time that 10% of the rocks contain fossils. The geologist selects twenty rocks from this area. a) State 2 conditions that must be apply in order for a binomial model to be valid. ( ) 2 Find the probability that in the geologist’s sample there will be … b) … one rock containing fossils. ( ) 2 c) … at least one rock containing fossils. ( ) 2 The geologist selects a new sample of n rocks. He wants to have at least a 95% chance that his new sample will contain fossils. d) Determine the smallest value of n . ( ) 5"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-r/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"In a clothing factory, the time taken by machines to manufacture a certain type of shirt, are assumed to be Normally distributed with a mean of 44 minutes and a standard deviation of 4 minutes. a) Determine the value of t , if 10.56% of the shirts take more than t minutes to be manufactured. ( ) 4 b) Find the probability that a shirt picked at random, took between 42 and 51 minutes to manufacture. ( ) 4 c) If a shirt took less than 45 minutes to be made, calculate the probability that it in fact took more than 42 minutes to make. ( ) 5 The owner of the factory believes that the mean time is greater than 44 minutes, due to the aging machinery. He finds that the mean manufacturing time of a random sample of 4 shirts to be 47 minutes.\" style=\"--madas-width:569.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"d)  By clearly stating suitable hypotheses, test at the 5% level of significance the owner&#x27;s belief. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Looking at the diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:588.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Let X ∼N(44, 16). (a) P(X ≤t) = 0.8944, [B1] [A1] giving t = 44 + 4Φ−1(0.8944) ≈49.0 min . [M1] [A1] (b) P(42 &lt; X &lt; 51) = Φ(1.75) −Φ(−0.5) ≈0.6514 . [M1] Standardise 42 [M1] standardise 51 [A1] subtract the cumulative probabilities [A1] evaluate (c) The numerator is P(42 &lt; X &lt; 45) and the denominator is P(X &lt; 45).\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The two endpoint scores are (42 −44)/4 = −0.5, (45 −44)/4 = 0.25. The conditional probability is [Φ(0.25) −Φ(−0.5)]/Φ(0.25) ≈0.4847 . [B1] Identify the conditioning event [B1] identify the overlap [M1] standardise its bounds [A1] express the ratio of probabilities [A1] evaluate (d) Setting hypotheses: Test H0 : µ = 44 [B1] against H1 : µ &gt; 44. [A1] For four independent shirts, the standard error under √ H0 is 4/ 4 = 2. The observed statistic is (47 −44)/2 = 1.5, [A1] giving p = 1 −Φ(1.5) ≈0.0668 &gt; 0.05. [A1] Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence that the mean exceeds 44 minutes: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 19 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "In a clothing factory, the time taken by machines to manufacture a certain type of shirt, are assumed to be Normally distributed with a mean of 44 minutes and a standard deviation of 4 minutes. a) Determine the value of t , if 10.56% of the shirts take more than t minutes to be manufactured. ( ) 4 b) Find the probability that a shirt picked at random, took between 42 and 51 minutes to manufacture. ( ) 4 c) If a shirt took less than 45 minutes to be made, calculate the probability that it in fact took more than 42 minutes to make. ( ) 5 The owner of the factory believes that the mean time is greater than 44 minutes, due to the aging machinery. He finds that the mean manufacturing time of a random sample of 4 shirts to be 47 minutes. d)  By clearly stating suitable hypotheses, test at the 5% level of significance the owner's belief. ( ) 6"
  },
