  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-r/q9",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"C A B x m 3 m The figure above shows a uniform wooden beam AB , of length x m and weight 80 N . The beam is smoothly hinged at A and rests in a horizontal position on a smooth support at C , where AC = 3 m . When a rock of weight 70 N is placed on the beam at B the magnitude of the reaction force on the beam at C is 165 N . The beam is modelled as a uniform rod and the rock as a particle.\" style=\"--madas-width:565.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-question-2.svg\" alt=\"a) Calculate the value of x . ( ) 3 b) Explain briefly the model … ( ) i. … the beam is a uniform rod. 1 ii. … the rock is a particle. ( ) 1 The rock is next moved to a new position D on the beam, so that the beam with the rock at D remains in equilibrium in a horizontal position. The magnitude of reaction force at the support at C is now twenty times as large as the reaction force at the hinge at A . c) Calculate the distance AD . ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:592.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Looking at the diagram: (a) Moments about A give 3(165) = 80(x/2) + 70x , [B1] = 110x hence x = 4.5 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) (i) The rod is treated as thin and its mass is evenly distributed along its length, so its weight acts at the midpoint: [M1] (ii) The rock’s size and rotational effects can be ignored: Its weight acts at its position on the beam: [M1] (c) Let C’s upward reaction be R. There are no applied horizontal forces, so the hinge reaction is vertical: [M1] If it acts upwards, R + R/20 = 150 and R = 1000/7. If it acts downwards, R −R/20 = 150 [A1] and R = 3000/19. With AD = d, moments about A give 3R = 80(2.25) + 70d. Thus d = 174/49 m ≈3.55 m or d = 558/133 m ≈4.20 m , [A1] respectively: For each reaction solve d = (3R −180)/70.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-r/q9-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The positive vertical reactions satisfy 21R = 3000 or 19R = 3000, according to which direction the hinge force acts: Both positions are on the beam: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:665.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 9 is 9 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "C A B x m 3 m The figure above shows a uniform wooden beam AB , of length x m and weight 80 N . The beam is smoothly hinged at A and rests in a horizontal position on a smooth support at C , where AC = 3 m . When a rock of weight 70 N is placed on the beam at B the magnitude of the reaction force on the beam at C is 165 N . The beam is modelled as a uniform rod and the rock as a particle. a) Calculate the value of x . ( ) 3 b) Explain briefly the model … ( ) i. … the beam is a uniform rod. 1 ii. … the rock is a particle. ( ) 1 The rock is next moved to a new position D on the beam, so that the beam with the rock at D remains in equilibrium in a horizontal position. The magnitude of reaction force at the support at C is now twenty times as large as the reaction force at the hinge at A . c) Calculate the distance AD . ( ) 4 "
